WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
- The mother of a child
who I do not have in class

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.220
set up a parent-teacher conference

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.866
so she could meet me to ask me on a date.

00:00:07.866 --> 00:00:10.449
(upbeat music)

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.980
- This was about four or five
years into my teaching career.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.270
And thus far, I'd only
been teaching fourth grade

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.320
but the school I was at

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.451
had an opening for the
kindergarten teacher position.

00:00:22.451 --> 00:00:24.640
If we know anything about kindergarteners,

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.950
everything is so new and
wonderful and exciting to them.

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.790
Anything from their lunch that
day, to the bird they see,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:36.620
all the way to farting or as
my class called it, tooting.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.010
So the way I would do my mornings
in my kindergarten class,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:41.500
is I would have our morning meeting,

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.100
we'd go over our ABC's,
today's date, then numbers

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.650
all that good stuff, and end it

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.210
up with a nice morning reading time.

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:51.220
They would always sit on this huge,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.720
colorful rug in front of me.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.550
And they sat in their assigned seats

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.220
and every now and then, you know,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.620
something would happen
where one of them would fart

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.260
and everyone would like
smile at each other.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.160
Well this day, I was
just so into my reading

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.300
and I was so excited and into my book,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.200
as I'm turning the page,
I lean a little bit and I,

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.330
yes, Miss Stanley, farts.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:14.800
And it's a pretty big part too.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.140
It's nothing small that
can just be covered up.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.540
Right, so immediately when it happens,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.300
I know it's so loud that
the kids hear it too.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.590
And I immediately think, oh,
we've gotta cover this up.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.700
So I said, who did that, who farted?

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.100
Just to save myself.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.210
And all the kids kind
of look at each other

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.340
and they start pointing
at each other, you know,

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.380
and they're like, no, I
didn't do it, I didn't do it.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.880
And so I just said, so remember
that if you have something

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.110
whenever you need to fart,
just go over in the corner

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.040
so we don't have to deal with
the smell or the sound of it.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.115
And one of the kids raises
his hand and he says, yeah,

00:01:46.115 --> 00:01:48.940
Miss. Stanley, that's a great idea.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:51.610
I think you should follow that rule too.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.550
The whole thing in my mind

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.190
to get my students to
not know that that was me

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.210
who farted completely crumbled.

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:00.850
I think a few of them realized it was me,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.320
especially the student who said that

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:03.630
but some of them still, you know,

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.040
to this day are probably
like, I wonder who that was?

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:07.890
Needless to say, pretty embarrassing.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.270
- I teach first graders.

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.270
And just like when I was in first grade,

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.610
my favorite part of the
day would be recess.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:16.800
It's just one of those times of day

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.670
that everyone looks forward to.

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:19.780
I look forward to it of course,

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:23.336
because it is my break time,
my time to rest, to relax.

00:02:23.336 --> 00:02:25.760
Of course, when we go outside though

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.240
the students always
challenge me to a race.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.130
Some of the students
in my class are saying,

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.277
Miss. Brown, Miss. Brown let's race,

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:33.780
let me beat you in this race.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.301
My response every day is, no, I'm tired.

00:02:37.301 --> 00:02:39.590
Go play with your friends.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.510
I would even have on my dress shoes

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:42.810
or I just wasn't in the mood.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:46.400
Well, this particular day,
I had on old tennis shoes.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.150
I was in a great mood.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.470
This time when they asked me to a race,

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:54.400
I mistakenly said yes, I
accepted the challenge.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:55.540
I was ready to go.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.400
So we had a student come over
to say the ready set, go.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.460
They wanted it to be as fair as possible.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:02.670
Well, I wasn't nervous

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.530
because my legs are longer than theirs.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:06.840
I played tennis before.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:09.580
I knew that I still had
some gumption in me.

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.030
Well, no, we start the race.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:15.140
The student says, ready, set, go.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:16.520
We start running, we start running.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.480
I have forgot that the reason I wore

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.210
my old tennis shoes that day

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.010
is because it had been raining a lot

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.080
for the past week and a half.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.050
So I'm racing, I'm running,
of course I'm ahead.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.020
I'm looking back, making
sure that they're good.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.380
As I'm looking back, I turn to the side,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.010
realize that I'm going straight
forward into a mud pile.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.020
The students have kind
of veered off from it.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.280
I fall right dab in the middle of it.

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.770
The mud is everywhere,
the students run over.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:46.710
They're like, are you okay, are you okay?

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:48.110
Of course, I'm looking over.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.080
There's mud all over
me, all over my clothes.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:52.060
It was just one of those moments

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:54.840
where you just wish that
everything would stop

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.860
and you could just turn back time.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.240
If I had said, no, I got up
dusted myself off, showed them

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.990
you know, no matter what
happens, you keep it moving.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.410
Keep pushing forward, it'll all be okay.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:08.303
But I did not race for
the rest of that year.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.300
- Let me start off by saying,
I am a very good teacher.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.220
So on top of being named one of the most

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.190
influential educators in America,

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.900
I was also named one of
the 25 fittest celebrities

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.120
in the world by Men's Fitness
magazine, which I'm not.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.160
And I was also named the
sexiest teacher alive

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:26.200
by People Magazine.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.110
As you can imagine,

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.800
those titles have led to
some uncomfortable moments.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:32.070
So this one day, I had a
parent teacher conference.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.590
Usually get a letter in
your mailbox that tells you

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.020
you have a parent teacher conference

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:37.520
and this is the parent
and this is the child.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.500
So I went and signed in in
the morning as I always do.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.840
I looked, I got this note.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.250
I didn't recognize the parent's name

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:44.070
but for the most part, sometimes obviously

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.530
our students have different last names

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:46.700
than their parents, so that's fine.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:48.370
I also didn't recognize
the student's name.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.080
And in fairness, it was probably
the third week of school.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.440
So I kind of look at it as, okay,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.610
well, I mean I obviously
have this student,

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:55.950
maybe he goes by a different name.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.500
Maybe I know him by a nickname.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.130
So that's why I didn't recognize his name.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.120
So I didn't think anything of it.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.470
Time comes for my conference.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:03.250
I go into guidance where we have them.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.700
I sit down, I meet the
mother and we're talking,

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:06.810
she's asked me questions.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.290
She's like, oh Mr. Ferroni, you know,

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:09.700
heard so many great things about you.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:10.720
And I'm like, that's great.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.570
It felt like more like
she was interviewing me

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.230
than it was a parent-teacher conference.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.376
So I said, I'm like, Mrs. so-and-so

00:05:16.376 --> 00:05:17.739
I'm like, just so you know, I'm like,

00:05:17.739 --> 00:05:18.572
I'm sorry to apologize

00:05:18.572 --> 00:05:19.980
but I don't recognize your son's name.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:21.420
What class does he have me?

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:23.080
Like what period class is he in of mine?

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.760
And that's when she said very simply,

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.270
oh you don't have my son in class.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:27.940
You don't have him, he's a junior here

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.020
but he has a different teacher.

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.980
And I was like, well then why
did you wanna meet with me?

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.530
She's like, I just wanted to meet you.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.990
She's like, I heard so
many things about you.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.260
I just actually just
wanted to meet Mr. Ferroni.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:38.090
She was like, I was
curious, are you single?

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:38.923
And I was at the time.

00:05:38.923 --> 00:05:40.560
And she's like, well, if you want,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.870
I would love to either
go have drinks with you

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:43.703
or go out to dinner.

00:05:43.703 --> 00:05:45.590
She's like, well, here's my
number if you change your mind.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.040
So she actually gave me her number,

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:50.400
the mother of a child who
I do not have in class

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.250
set up a parent-teacher conference

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:54.500
so she could meet me to ask me on a date.

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.790
And again, as flattering as
it was, it was prep period.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.240
I just thought it was so inappropriate

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.680
and so awkward to do it
during that time period,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.013
prep periods are valuable.

